EXT. RAILWAY CUTTING, FLOODED TRENCH LINE - PRE-DAWN

Mud. Barbed wire. A rusted boxcar tipped on its side, half sunk. Thin mist over the standing water.

SERGEANT BORIS KALININ-ADEYEMI crouches in the doorway of the boxcar. Tall. Gray at the temples. A cigarette he does not light.

Behind him, PRIVATE LUCIEN MAROIS (19) sits against the wall with his knees to his chest. His rifle lies across his lap. He stares at the floor.

Further down the car, CORPORAL TAMSIN BRENNAN-OKORO crouches over a field kit. She counts bullets into her palm. Counts again.

Silence. Water drips.

BORIS
Marois.

Lucien does not look up.

BORIS (CONT'D)
Marois.

Lucien lifts his head. His eyes are very bright.

LUCIEN
Sergeant.

BORIS
Eat something.

LUCIEN
Not hungry.

Boris tosses a tin of biscuits across the car. Lucien catches it against his chest without opening it.

TAMSIN
He'll eat when he's ready.

BORIS
He'll eat now.

Tamsin glances at the boy, then at Boris. She says nothing. Lucien cracks the tin, takes one biscuit, and holds it without biting.

Far off, a shell lands. The boxcar shivers. A fine rain of rust falls from the roof. No one flinches. Tamsin finishes counting. Boris lights the cigarette at last, takes one drag, then pinches it out and returns it to his pocket.

TAMSIN
Eleven rounds each.

BORIS
That's enough.

TAMSIN
It's not enough, Boris.

BORIS
It is for what we've got.

A second shell. Closer. Lucien bites the biscuit. Chews. Swallows.

LUCIEN
Sergeant?

BORIS
Yes.

LUCIEN
Is my brother's company still on the ridge?

Boris looks at Tamsin. Tamsin looks at her hands. It is the only answer, and everyone in the boxcar understands it.

BORIS
Eat the other half.

Lucien does. He keeps his eyes on his sergeant the whole time.

A third shell, much farther away. The mist begins to thin. Pale light slides along the water.

BORIS (CONT'D)
(quietly)
When it's quiet, we move.

TAMSIN ^
(quietly)
When it's quiet, we move.

Boris almost smiles. He gathers his rifle. Tamsin shoulders her kit. Lucien stands, unsteady, and the three of them wait in the doorway of the tipped boxcar for the guns to stop.
